## 6. License keys and feature entitlements

A license key records a technical entitlement; it is not a substitute for a copyright license or commercial agreement.

Subject to eligibility under Section 2, the following entitlements apply to the current Kudurru licensing model:

| Capability | No key | Free Personal/Nonprofit Key | Business Key |
|---|---|---|---|
| DNS answering, blocking, authoritative zones, block page, dashboard, short-term statistics, API tokens, and built-in administrator | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Kudurru nodes in one cluster | 1 | Up to 3 | As specified in commercial agreement |
| DNS policies | One seeded default policy | Unlimited | Unlimited |
| Additional role bindings, scoped RBAC, Kubernetes RBAC (`ee/rbac`) | No | Yes | Yes |
| OIDC single sign-on (`ee/oidc`) | No | Yes | Yes |
| Stored audit events and export (`ee/auditstore`) | No | Yes | Yes |
| Statistics older than 24 hours (`ee/longterm`) | No | Yes | Yes |
| Unblock requests and review workflow | No | Yes | Yes |
| dnsdist/keepalived edge and VIP integration (`ee/edge`) | No | Yes | Yes |
| Integrations such as external-dns (`ee/integration`) | No | Yes | Yes |
| Business or government production-use rights | No | No | Only as granted by commercial agreement |

Business-key capabilities are limited to the plan, modules, quantities, and term authorized by the accepted commercial agreement and Order Form. A Commercial Core purchase does not automatically authorize `ee/` modules; the Order Form must include the Enterprise plan or named modules.

A “Node” is an independently running Kudurru server instance participating in a deployment or cluster. Separately licensed edge programs that do not run Kudurru itself are not Kudurru Nodes.

A free key does not authorize more than three Kudurru Nodes in one cluster unless the Licensor expressly authorizes an exception in writing.

A nonprofit may receive the same free technical entitlements as a personal user. Keys may contain additional identifying, validity, or deployment-scope limitations communicated at issuance.

Possessing source code or changing the license-checking code does not expand the rights or entitlements granted here. Modifications for legitimate development, interoperability testing, and security research remain permitted under Section 2.3; using them to exceed deployment entitlements is not.

## 7. Safety of DNS operations and key expiration

Kudurru's license enforcement is designed not to interrupt DNS answering.

Missing, invalid, or expired keys may limit management-plane capabilities, cluster configuration writes, and initiation of gated features; they do not intentionally disable existing DNS responses.

The documented implementation provides a 30-day technical grace period for an expired key and does not stop an already running dnsdist/keepalived edge process solely because its key later expires.

License-key verification is performed locally without a required online license check.

These technical safety behaviors do not extend an expired commercial agreement, increase a node entitlement, or grant otherwise prohibited commercial or government use.

A commercial customer's contractual renewal, expiration, and remedy provisions are governed by its commercial agreement.

